WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.680
They call this the wooden

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.423
house district of Helsinki.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.510
These beauties were built
back in the early 1900s

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:13.939
so that the workers of Finland

00:00:13.939 --> 00:00:16.750
could have nice places to live

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.083
and nice gardens to tend.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.830
They're symbolic of Finland's famed

00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:25.640
social support system that has made Finns

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:27.593
some of the happiest people on Earth.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.523
Bless you, Finland, and
your wheelbarrow gardens.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.493
Of course, progress
often has its own plans.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:40.530
The workers here, for example, have been

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.856
replaced by artsy hipsters

00:00:42.856 --> 00:00:44.933
seeking a trendy neighborhood.

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:49.670
And, soon enough, workers and
hipsters all over the world

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.870
might be replaced by
robots that don't care

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:56.503
about social systems or
pretty gardens at all.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.173
Hello robot overlords.

00:01:15.996 --> 00:01:18.270
Here at Helsinki's most
scenic garbage dump

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.300
are some hard-working robotic arms

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.000
from a company called ZenRobotics.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.640
The Zen part obviously being
some bizarre marketing ploy

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:30.747
because there's nothing
calming about these things.

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.250
Their mission is to divvy
up industrial trash,

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:40.100
sorting things like wood and
metal into their own piles.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.810
How do you get the robot
to see that it's not wood,

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:45.630
that it's a piece of steel, a plastic bag?

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:48.210
All this stuff is so
amorphous and different.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.700
We use cameras, we use metal detectors,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.040
we use 3D sensors, and we use
also near infrared sensors.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:57.970
The A.I. is able then to predict, okay,

00:01:57.970 --> 00:01:59.253
what kind of material?

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.000
We tried to understand
how is a human operator

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.490
sorting waste, and he is not picking it

00:02:09.490 --> 00:02:10.670
and putting it here.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:12.020
It picks and throws.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:12.853
Yeah.

00:02:12.853 --> 00:02:15.000
And we simulated the same movements,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.853
so our robot opens the grip
and lets the object fly.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:21.570
And what do we know about

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.650
how accurate the robots are?

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:25.800
Depending on the different kind of waste,

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.674
we can go to a purity up to 90%.

00:02:29.674 --> 00:02:31.350
Can we go take a closer
look at the robots?

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:34.427
Yes, for sure, yes, let's go.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:42.830
So, the waste comes up,
goes on this conveyor belt,

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.391
and then this is where
it's getting scanned--

00:02:44.391 --> 00:02:45.580
Yes.

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.900
And then in this moment
where it's getting scanned,

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.160
it's telling the robots down the line,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.150
there's gonna be an object
coming that I want you to grab.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:54.000
Yes.

00:02:55.928 --> 00:02:58.220
And then the arms start to go to work.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.160
Yes.

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:01.360
The robot gets to pick which waste

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:03.157
and to throw it in which bin.

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:05.790
I mean, it does a really good job to me

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.490
of picking up these objects
that are such different sizes.

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:10.600
Yep.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:13.438
You can believe there are
many man hours invested

00:03:13.438 --> 00:03:15.530
to really develop a gripper, what is

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:18.460
able to grip totally
different kind of sizes,

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.111
shapes and waste.

00:03:20.111 --> 00:03:23.833
And I think that was one
of the biggest challenges.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.830
ZenRobotics recently put its robots

00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.880
to work right here in the
heart of Silicon Valley

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:40.593
at this massive garbage
processing facility in San Jose.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.480
Artificial Intelligence and trash

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:48.430
commingling in harmony.

00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:50.113
Just the way nature intended.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.880
Back in Finland, I took a
drive from the dump to Espoo.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.070
It's a city about 25
minutes outside of Helsinki

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:05.820
that is something of a tech suburb.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.023
Nokia's once glorious
headquarters are here,

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.123
and so too are its more
sedate current headquarters.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.400
And the Angry Birds are here too.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.833
But I have not come to
Espoo for disgruntled birds

00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:27.703
or airborne pigs.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.413
I have come to see some satellites.

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.623
This is the headquarters of Iceye.

00:04:45.751 --> 00:04:48.210
It is one of a handful of
start-ups that have built small

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:50.630
satellites that take constant pictures

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.080
of what's happening on Earth.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:55.430
How many satellites have
you guys put up today?

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.240
The commercial constellation
that we operate right now

00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:58.073
is three satellites.

00:04:58.073 --> 00:04:58.970
Three satellites.

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:02.540
And then, your satellites,
they're mini fridge sized?

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.240
The famous mini fridge, yeah!

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:06.780
And then you also want to try and surround

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:10.440
the earth with dozens,
hundreds of these things?

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.340
Right, yeah.

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:12.990
So, we really want to make the system,

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:16.360
that allows you to sort of
reliably, and accurately,

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:20.490
and objectively see
everything at all times.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:25.030
It's almost like having
a sort of a MRI scan

00:05:25.030 --> 00:05:26.043
for the earth.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:35.140
Iceye satellites travel from pole to pole

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.723
every 45 minutes.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.870
Rather than cameras,
their three small sats

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:43.480
use something called
synthetic aperture radar,

00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.590
or SAR, to pound the earth
with microwave signals

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:47.683
from low earth orbit.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.620
They then use signals
that are reflected back

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.120
from the earth to build
images of the surface.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:57.570
Unlike cameras, SAR can see
through the cloudiest of days

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.033
and the darkest of nights.

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.010
Iceye combine this radar technology

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:05.940
with advanced image
processing and computer vision

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.010
software to create
highly detailed pictures.

00:06:10.010 --> 00:06:11.540
Can we peak at some of these images, and--

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:12.373
Sure.

00:06:12.373 --> 00:06:13.720
Walk through what you guys do?

00:06:16.550 --> 00:06:19.626
So, this was an example
of the hurricane Dorian,

00:06:19.626 --> 00:06:20.913
in the Bahamas.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:28.300
We were able to image this exactly when

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.250
the hurricane was on top of the island.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.910
This is the island as it normally is.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.390
And then, in the afterwards,

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:40.633
the land border used to be here.

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.840
And I think here, the really
dramatic thing is that

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:48.970
all of these red dots are
buildings fully submerged.

00:06:48.970 --> 00:06:52.848
This is exactly what becomes
insurance information to--

00:06:52.848 --> 00:06:53.681
Yeah, so, you have proof that all

00:06:53.681 --> 00:06:55.200
of this stuff flooded, and--

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.033
Yeah.

00:06:56.033 --> 00:06:57.630
And proof is one thing,
and then, of course,

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:58.830
just the ability to react.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:01.470
Like, now what if you
could trigger the payment

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.540
to these guys automatically?

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:05.590
So that people get to rebuild their lives,

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:06.423
and so forth.

00:07:06.423 --> 00:07:07.723
Much more quickly, yeah, yeah.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:16.340
Now here, we're looking at a
large tanker, The Grace One.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.803
It relates to the sanctions to Iran.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:21.930
It became this big story,
when it was impounded

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:24.900
in Gibraltar, when it was
said that it was headed

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.923
towards Syria, with a
tank full of Iranian oil.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:32.410
This is an image from a while back

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.550
where we're able to see
the Grace One ship here,

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.423
in the Iranian shores in January.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:43.190
So, maybe the US knows
where this tanker is.

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:45.140
The NSA knows, the CIA.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.760
And they can choose to
make public what they want.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:50.930
But, basically, with you guys,
there's a democratization

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:52.960
to all this, where anyone
who's willing to pay

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.160
for your imagery, you know?

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:55.993
Yeah.

00:07:55.993 --> 00:07:57.630
It's not just in the hands
of these few governments now.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:00.560
It's like everybody can
know what's going on.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:04.480
Yeah, I think that's part
of the big story here.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:08.060
In the case of this tanker,
what was really interesting is,

00:08:08.060 --> 00:08:09.700
was it full or was it not full?

00:08:09.700 --> 00:08:11.483
How full was it? And so forth.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.100
If it's full of oil,
it's down in the water,

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.030
and you're getting something on the depth,

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:19.372
from like a shadow off the
water, or something like that.

00:08:19.372 --> 00:08:22.333
Yeah, exactly like this.

00:08:22.333 --> 00:08:25.940
And then this is a mine,
it's an open pit mine.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:29.960
So, the more mundane use
case here is just that

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.880
you monitor the progress
of the mining activity.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:36.030
But, of course, the
safety of, is there some

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:39.120
displacement that is
likely to cause landslides?

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:40.700
Or, is there some underground mining that

00:08:40.700 --> 00:08:42.543
is likely to cause collapses?

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:48.550
When it started in 2014,

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.330
Iceye was the very first company

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.523
to build a commercial
satellite in Finland.

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.960
Since then, it's raised
more than 65 million dollars

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.900
from investors and plans
to launch a constellation

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:07.950
of 18 small satellites in
the next couple of years.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:10.900
Right now, we're fully
booked with customers.

00:09:10.900 --> 00:09:12.600
So, there will be some governments.

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:16.130
There will be some
insurance, some finance.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:18.670
I mean, there's a ton
of positive use cases

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:20.070
to all this stuff.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.548
I do think some people, though,
would be creeped out a bit,

00:09:22.548 --> 00:09:24.620
about this all-seeing eye,

00:09:24.620 --> 00:09:27.700
that is keeping track of
what people are doing.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:30.260
I think, as far as the sort of creepiness,

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.560
and sort of personal level,

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:35.225
I think a lot of the
mobile phones, internet,

00:09:35.225 --> 00:09:39.402
Google type services,
they tend to track you

00:09:39.402 --> 00:09:42.890
to much higher precision, than we ever,

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.484
so, like we never would never really

00:09:45.484 --> 00:09:48.820
be in the business of identifying humans.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.950
We don't image faces.

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:53.573
The resolution is way lower than that.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.590
For us, it's about objectivity,

00:09:56.590 --> 00:09:58.790
of being able to provide as neutral

00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.590
a source of information as possible.

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.350
AI robots and super fancy satellites

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:11.450
are all well and good.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:13.243
Probably?

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.676
But no visit to Finland would be complete

00:10:18.676 --> 00:10:22.493
without exploring its
oldest and most famous tech:

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:24.960
the sauna.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.513
Or, as they say it here, the sauna.

00:10:31.540 --> 00:10:33.530
And since Finland is a tiny place full

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.220
of accommodating people,

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:38.150
I was able to find not just a sauna

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:39.980
but the sauna.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.150
And not just any sauna companion

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:44.730
but the sauna companion.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:48.297
Finland's most famous
actor, Jasper Paakkonen.

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.760
Along with having an outstanding six-pack,

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.313
Jasper owns this beauty.

00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:57.890
He joined me at about 11 p.m.

00:10:57.890 --> 00:10:59.800
to teach me the art of the sauna

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:03.020
and what it means to the Finnish people.

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.100
When you walk into a
sauna, you're stripped down

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:10.250
from your clothes,
you're stripped down from

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.210
all the titles, your wealth.

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:14.150
You leave your wallet outside

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:18.227
and it's just a bunch of
people at their most bare

00:11:18.227 --> 00:11:19.630
state of being.

00:11:19.630 --> 00:11:22.750
And it's really hard to not be honest

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:24.720
when you're so exposed and so bare.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:25.553
Yeah.

00:11:25.553 --> 00:11:26.960
So, this is a pretty common thing,

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:29.200
is to come in here and hash things out?

00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:30.050
Yeah.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:33.010
Sauna is the only word
from Finnish language

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:35.810
to travel to other
languages as a common word

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:39.510
to use and saunas are more common in Fin,

00:11:39.510 --> 00:11:42.980
it's more common to own a
sauna than it is to own a car.

00:11:42.980 --> 00:11:45.100
Which means every single
person has a sauna.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.040
Yeah.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:47.910
Everybody has these
saunas, but the ones here

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:50.970
are very traditional and what is the

00:11:50.970 --> 00:11:52.750
classic Finnish sauna?

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:55.740
The real sauna would always
be a wood-heated sauna

00:11:57.063 --> 00:12:00.487
because the burning of the
wood gives the sauna its,

00:12:01.370 --> 00:12:05.030
this very distinct flavor or this loyly.

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:07.947
Loyly the word means like
the spirit of the sauna

00:12:07.947 --> 00:12:09.700
and the feel when you throw water

00:12:09.700 --> 00:12:12.330
onto the sauna heater's rocks.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.580
That's loyly and you just
get much better loyly

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:17.491
when you heat it with wood.

00:12:17.491 --> 00:12:19.459
Loyly.
Loyly.

00:12:19.459 --> 00:12:20.792
Loyly.
Loyly.

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:30.160
Perhaps unimpressed by my grasp

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:32.170
of the Finnish language,

00:12:32.170 --> 00:12:33.880
Jasper decided it was a good idea

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:36.110
to complete the sauna experience

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:40.033
by subjecting me to a
ritualized form of icy torture.

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:44.600
We just lower ourselves down to our necks,

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:48.430
and then we take a few deep,
kind of just easy breaths.

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:49.781
Try not to hyperventilate.

00:12:49.781 --> 00:12:50.614
Okay.

00:13:01.559 --> 00:13:04.540
Takes a second to settle in.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:05.373
I feel good.

00:13:07.209 --> 00:13:09.510
Wooh!

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.290
Okay, that's refreshing.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:14.533
All right, man.

00:13:14.533 --> 00:13:18.790
See now, you start feeling
this tingling, warm...

00:13:18.790 --> 00:13:22.442
Definitely able to breathe
now, so that's positive.

00:13:22.442 --> 00:13:23.340
And you don't feel cold, right?

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.053
No, I feel good actually, yes.

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:29.183
Once you do it a few times,
first you get used to it,

00:13:30.070 --> 00:13:32.490
and then you get addicted to it.

00:13:32.490 --> 00:13:33.820
So, how many times would you do that

00:13:33.820 --> 00:13:35.770
during the course of a sauna session?

00:13:35.770 --> 00:13:38.110
I usually do it four times back and forth.

00:13:38.110 --> 00:13:39.880
So, sauna, swim, sauna, swim.

00:13:39.880 --> 00:13:42.600
And then you end it with the swim.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:46.090
I often say that sauna is like the church

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:47.970
for a normal, average Finn.

00:13:47.970 --> 00:13:50.281
If he or she goes to the sauna,

00:13:50.281 --> 00:13:54.230
you're in silence and you
sweat out the daily sorrows,

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:56.350
both mentally and physically.

00:13:56.350 --> 00:13:58.650
There's something church-like.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:13:59.770
Sadly, I feel like our version

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:02.237
is like a football game or something!

